"""
Solar irradiation for a fixed geographic location and date — a clear-sky model.

Ported from `ShipSIM.Components.Others.Solar.SolarIrradiation`.

The original Modelica model:
- Maintained a `discrete Integer dayOfYear` that incremented via `when` events when local
  hour wrapped past 24.
- Read an optional `tableOnFile` of measured irradiance to compute a cloud-correction
  ratio (`SolarIrradiationRatio`).
- Provided three signals: `IrradianceOnPanel(W/m²)`, `SunHeight(deg)`, `SunVector[3]`.

This Dyad port:
- Drops the discrete day-of-year counter and `when` event; the day of the year is fixed
  per simulation via `day_of_year` (1..365). The starting reference is treated as
  midnight of that day in the local time zone.
- Drops the optional measured-irradiance table; the output is the clear-sky reference
  irradiance directly. If the user wants cloud correction, multiply `IrradianceOnPanel`
  by an externally-supplied `SolarIrradiationRatio` real input downstream.
- Uses Julia helpers `DyadShip.sun_height_deg(...)` and `DyadShip.sun_vector_world(...)`
  which encode the same astronomy formulae as upstream (declination, true mean time,
  hour angle, altitude, azimuth).
- Exposes `IrradianceOnPanel` only when `Calculation_on_Panel = true` AND the panel
  inclination + azimuth inputs are wired. For now this port always uses a horizontal
  panel; an inclined-plane variant follows the same shape.

Limitations:
- Astronomy formulae preserved verbatim; no atmospheric extinction model beyond the
  reference irradiance scale.
- 3-vector inputs/outputs are exposed as triples of scalar `RealOutput`s, since Dyad
  does not have array connectors.
"""
component SolarIrradiation
  "Latitude in radians (positive North). Default ≈ 43.4° N (Galicia, Spain)."
  parameter latitude::Angle = 0.7579392485634024
  "Longitude in radians (positive East). Default ≈ -8.24° (Galicia)."
  parameter longitude::Angle = -0.1438785943756596
  "Time zone offset from UTC in hours."
  parameter time_zone::Real = 1
  "Day of year (1..365). Defaults to summer solstice."
  parameter day_of_year::Real = 172
  "Reference clear-sky horizontal irradiance [W/m²]."
  parameter irradiance_ref::Real = 1000

  SunHeight = RealOutput()
  SunVector_x = RealOutput()
  SunVector_y = RealOutput()
  SunVector_z = RealOutput()
  IrradianceOnPanel = RealOutput()
  IsDay = RealOutput()    # 1.0 when sun is above horizon, 0 otherwise

  variable sv::Real[3]
relations
  SunHeight = sun_height_deg(time, latitude, longitude, day_of_year, time_zone)
  sv = sun_vector_world(time, latitude, longitude, day_of_year, time_zone, irradiance_ref)
  SunVector_x = sv[1]
  SunVector_y = sv[2]
  SunVector_z = sv[3]
  # Horizontal-panel irradiance equals the magnitude of the vertical (Z) component of
  # the sun ray (since the panel normal is +Z). Negative because SunVector points from
  # sun to ground (downward when above horizon).
  IrradianceOnPanel = ifelse(SunHeight > 0, -sv[3], 0)
  IsDay = ifelse(SunHeight > 0, 1, 0)
end
